uld avoid complex  program or delivery dependencies  avoid target backend systems that  require significant refactoring  avoid use cases where the user base or  customer demand does not already exist  it should be engaging so make sure your  project is engaging  you need to deliver something of clear  business value and impact aligned to  strategic goals and sponsor interest  it should be it should deliver strategic  value deliver business value  it should resonate with your audience  and it should create a pathway for  repeatable success  your mvbo should be sufficiently  difficult the apis delivered should not  just be pass-throughs they should be  complex enough  to demonstrate key api management  benefits including  controlled access to resources and  assets analytics  self-service consumption by a developer  portal  and they should demonstrate how they'll  scale so consider all those things when  you're thinking about an mvbo  next let's look at three tips in the  product category  tip number five standardize the security  backend services often have different  protocols for authentication  depending on when they were developed  navigating those multiple security  protocols causes extra work for api  consumers  consider standardizing the security you  present to your api consumers they'll be  much happier and they'll thank you for  this  an api platform can help you standardize  your security in addition to policies  for authentication  there's policies for rate limiting and  quotas payload protection and more  your api platform can control access to  protected resources  to help you with compliance also you may  need to tie into an existing identity  management system this is an example of  a flow that  generates an oauth token after users  enter their passwords  the authentication flow makes a call out  to the identity system to verify the  user's username and password  and if the credentials are accepted it  returns a token to the application that  is then s