uld avoid complex  program or delivery dependencies  avoid target backend systems that  require significant refactoring  avoid use cases where the user base or  customer demand does not already exist  it should be engaging so make sure your  project is engaging  you need to deliver something of clear  business value and impact aligned to  strategic goals and sponsor interest  it should be it should deliver strategic  value deliver business value  it should resonate with your audience  and it should create a pathway for  repeatable success  your mvbo should be sufficiently  difficult the apis delivered should not  just be pass-throughs they should be  complex enough  to demonstrate key api management  benefits including  controlled access to resources and  assets analytics  self-service consumption by a developer  portal  and they should demonstrate how they'll  scale so consider all those things when  you're thinking about an mvbo  next let's look at three tips in the  product category  tip number five standardize the security  backend services often have different  protocols for authentication  depending on when they were developed  navigating those multiple security  protocols causes extra work for api  consumers  consider standardizing the security you  present to your api consumers they'll be  much happier and they'll thank you for  this  an api platform can help you standardize  your security in addition to policies  for authentication  there's policies for rate limiting and  quotas payload protection and more  your api platform can control access to  protected resources  to help you with compliance also you may  need to tie into an existing identity  management system this is an example of  a flow that  generates an oauth token after users  enter their passwords  the authentication flow makes a call out  to the identity system to verify the  user's username and password  and if the credentials are accepted it  returns a token to the application that  is then sent to the business flow  so simple example of how you might  integrate with an existing  identity management system number six  measure monitor and improve so how do  you improve your api program  measuring how applications call it and  monitoring the performance will help you  know  what needs to be tuned and give you data  on how your program is doing  so after you've built a few apis and  your application developers have  discovered them  and started to send you traffic be great  if you could measure your api program  the analytics system in apigee edge  allows you to measure every aspect of  your api traffic you can measure  operational details like response time  frequency of different types of errors  you can find things that are interesting  to your business  like where are people located when they  call your apis or which applications are  sending the most traffic  maybe it's interesting to see what type  of client they're using are they on  internet explorer are they on chrome are  they on different mobile platforms  all of these things can be answered with  analytics reports  so what should you be measuring here's a  list of some of the common metrics that  we've heard from customers that they  find valuable  so think about the number of apis the  number of developers the number of  customers  number of partners the number of apps  the speed to api  speed to onboard so in other words how  long does it take to get up and going  traffic growth business breadth cost  reduction direct revenue  and net promoter score apogee has some  great reports to help you understand  your api program for example  here is an engagement report that shows  you how many developers are using your  platform and their level of engagement  so one final important aspect of  operating an api platform is ensuring  the availability of your apis  apigee launched apr monitoring product a  couple weeks ago  freely available now to all cloud-based  enterprise edge customers  by the apis and the applications that  use them make sure your apis deliver  value either directly or indirectly  consider measuring your rate of  innovation or your time to app  while an api may not directly generate  revenue what value  does it bring if you can create  applications in a month instead of a  year  can apis provide you with new sources of  value do you have unique data  that might be valuable to others can you  charge for it what new channels of  business  can you explore accuweather is able to  monetize their data by packaging it into  different offerings with different price  points  so think of approaches for how you might  want to do this  next let's shift to infrastructure and  devops i have four tips in this category  tip number eight automate and integrate  the ui is great but  automation is even better most users  start off with the ui  but they'll move to automating parts of  their processes apogee has a great set  of management apis that allow you to  automate every aspect of the platform  take a look at the reference docs and  start exploring these i've got the url  on the screen here  the apigee community is a great place to  find advice and resources for ci cd  automation there's a lot of people  who've gone through this before you  and there's links to tools and  discussion of how to use them  number nine grow with microservices  microservices enable developers to build  and deploy software with speed agility  and scale necessary to meet the needs of  your business  you can decompose a monolithic  application into a set of services that  can be developed  and released independently  microservices are an architecture  approach to software development where  software is composed of small  independent services that communicate  over well-defined apis  microservices enable modern enterprises  to create a flexible architecture where  services can be versioned deployed  and scaled without impact to other  systems or applications  so the great thing about microservices  is they can easily grow with your  business  but that comes with some new  complexities an app development team  may rush to implement microservices and  when they do this they inadvertently  create silos with inconsistencies in  security visibility documentation and  governance  many of the apis that connect  microservices are not secured  consistently across the organization  so these microservices and associate  apis are often difficult to reuse  analyze or even discover for use by  their teams you can solve this problem  by managing the consumption of your  microservices  with your api platform so number 10  let's talk about cloud migration  migrating to the cloud isn't  a straight path enterprises adopt  different paths to define their cloud  strategy often enterprises will pick  multiple paths based on different  projects or workloads  so there's three key takeaways from this  slide migration paths will vary  even within the company and across  applications migration  migrations are not instant some take  months or even years  across all the paths of migration api  management can play a key role  in making integrations easier so how can  api management help  so a few different things to look at  here apogee can help with many of these  different challenges  service connectivity one of the primary  concerns of app migration is tight  coupling of the clients or user  experiences to the backend services or  apps  apogee acts as an abstraction layer  between the exposure and consumption  allowing for service connectivity  while parts of the app's back end or  front end are migrated  to the cloud security enforcement apigee  can act both as a security enforcement  layer as well as a security mediation  layer  as apps are modernized while moving to  the cloud it's very common  for the modern apps to have a different  security mechanism like jwt or oauth  versus the legacy traditional back ends  like saml basic auth etc apogee can  bring in uniformity by allowing the  modern apps  to use one common mechanism while  mediating the credentials while talking  to the legacy back-ends and vice versa  apigee can also make sure that security  mechanisms like regex checks  dlp enforcement are done while on-prem  apps  that have strict compliance needs are  calling  the cloud workloads for visibility  by acting as the facade across on-prem  gcp workloads apigee can provide  visibility into how app migrations are  happening  and the success or traction for the  migrated apps the api metrics that  apigee provides are slightly higher  level as apigee will have visibility  into which apps developer  clients are calling your api which api  product is being used to call the api  and etc  the api metrics can be pulled in through  apogee's management api and analyzed  along with other operational metrics  collected by by the app's logging  framework etc  api facades so most often not all of the  apps can be migrated at once  the re-platformed or modernized apps  will have to talk to a monolith or  legacy  or traditional backend these back-ends  most of the times will be in legacy  protocols like soap  xml it's not ideal for modern apps to  consume them that way and it adds the  additional burden of maintaining  additional libraries making the app  heavier and complex  apigee can help create api facades that  are modern while keeping the monolith  intact  the re-platformed or modern apps can  talk to these facades instead of going  directly to the back end  and finally service management lift and  modernization is one of the standard  themes of app migration  microservices often on kubernetes is a  vehicle for app modernization as  enterprises realize the success of  microservices-based architectures  the number of microservices explodes and  the apps are migrated to cloud  without the right security and  visibility it's extremely difficult to  accelerate the adoption of microservices  some enterprises are already already  looking at istio to solve this problem  for customers who have adopted istio  apogee can provide  enterprise support for non-gcp istio  implementations as well as add the  higher level  api management aspects like oauth  through the mixer adapter  for customers who are not looking at sdl  example customers  who are on pcf apogee's microgateway can  add similar capabilities  and manage their microservices like  security and analytics  so that's the top ten list next up let's  take some questions  okay let's start off with the first  question where should i start with the  management apis  how can they be used to automate my  deployment so  take a look at the reference section of  the api documentation  for apigee and in there you'll see a  list of all of those  pick one or two and play with it and  just get a feel for how it works  one thing people will commonly do is  automate the deployment process  so meaning if you're going to push  something for example from  dev to test or from maybe tests or qa  into production  you can automate that so take a look at  the the management api specifically  around that area  and give that a try next question has to  deal with  analytics and measuring your apis and  the question there is  how can i measure something business  specific with a custom report in apigee  so there's a statistics collector policy  in there the statistics collector policy  lets you grab anything  that's a variable in apogee so anything  from the request to the response or  anything you want to extract out of  there  you can save that as a custom metric and  that can be used in the reporting system  so you can combine that with the  built-in analytics build a custom report  and measure something that's interesting  so  think about what you might what you  might want to measure with that  okay next question you touched on  experience apis  what is an experience api so think of an  experience  as a an application or how somebody  would  experience your your services  so a lot of times we'll build custom  services just for  a particular application like you might  have a an experience api  to support a mobile application and  what's nice about having an api platform  for this is it lets you  easily build a custom api that's  specific to the experience that you're  trying to share  so next one in here is a question about  getting started with using apis for  consuming microservices  so what are the best practices and how  do you get started with this  so i'm going to go back to that outside  in type of thinking  so think about how we want to control  how people experience these  microservices  a great place to put apogee is right on  top of those microservices and it would  be used to  control access for external consumers  so put apogee in there and treat the  back end like you would any other back  end only it's going to be microservices  in this case  so what about measuring the number of  active subscriptions  uh that's a great idea so that is a good  metric to  to measure so you could do that through  some of the built-in reports that are uh  that are in apogee there's particularly  there's a developer engagement one  that'll show you who's subscribed and  what they're doing and things like that  there's a question in here uh where the  the listener  missed some bits of authentication  what's the best way to integrate with  vendors like octa  sso for oauth or abstract from end users  so there's a great webcast out there  that you may have seen last year take a  look out on  the youtube channel for apogee i've got  another webcast out there that just  deals with that topic  so basically there are policies inside  of apogee that will generate  oauth tokens and what you'll do is the  generation of that token  if you just put that in your flow it's  just going to generate a token  typically what people will do is they're  going to put some logic in front of that  to generate or not to generate to call  out to the identity management system  and verify the user in some way and then  if and only if they're good  will they generate the token so that's  the general pattern in there but there's  a whole webcast on that take a look on  the youtube channel  and you'll see under the apogee up close  series  there is a webcast just covering that  topic there's a question in here  transitioning to the api world is  disruptive to existing internal dev  community  what tips can you give for elevating  internal dev community learning  how best to promote and encourage so i  would explore  um why is it disruptive to move to the  to the api world and what is it about  that  um some of the things we'd focus on is  what's the onboarding process for apis  so is it easier if you have  an api management platform in place is  there a developer portal where they can  go and find examples and things like  that  so it it should be something the  developers are excited about and it  should be something that can be done  in a way that it's not too disruptive so  explore where the friction is in there  and work on that  so what best describes the attributes of  an api or a digital team  so we'll rephrase this as as kind of  who's on that team or  what they're doing so when you're first  getting started this might be a smaller  team there might be people on there who  are going to be  building apis and those might be people  who build web services in the past or at  least they have a knowledge of what the  back end services are  and and can look at those and figure out  what to expose  over time we'd like to add an api  product manager  because we want to think about these  things as products manage their  lifecycle and things like that so that's  going to be somebody who's a little bit  more business focused you may have some  people on there who are viewing reports  and measuring things uh there they'd  probably fall under that  that product manager role also you may  have some  people in there who are going to be  consuming these apis  but the team varies quite a bit um  you'll find a lot of different people on  the api team  there's a question here for enterprises  with b2b partners can apigee play an  outbound api  call management role management of calls  originating from originating from  inside so yeah we do see this so the  pattern here is that you can manage any  kind of api call so  maybe you're calling out or maybe you're  receiving calls coming in  either way you can think of this as a  good place to have api management if you  want to be able to measure that traffic  if you want to be able to  control it reshape requests and things  like that  you can use it for inside calls or for  outside calls and whether you're calling  in or out  question here is is there such a thing  as a private internal api  if yes how is it treated differently so  uh  private or internal api i might think of  that as an experience api and  and really the only thing that's going  to make it private versus public  is how you choose to expose it and um  you know who has access to it and and  who can use it  but we see this commonly we'll see  people have uh completely  uh you know big internal api programs at  their companies  where they're they're developing all  these things and none of it's exposed  externally  so yeah that's a very common use case  next question can apigee support custom  transformations also  does apogee support api orchestration so  for custom transformations yes very much  so so we have built-in  transformations in there for json to xml  xml to json  you can also use xslt in there if you're  working with xml  and there's support for using different  languages in there for custom policies  so  java javascript and python can be used  and they have complete control over the  request and the response flow so one  thing that's very common is  you might write a little bit of  javascript or java code in there and  that would  transform the response that comes back  that's that's a common use case  on the second side of that can apogee  support api orchestration  yes that's a common use case so you're  going to have one call in and then you  might call two or three different  services on the back end  collect those responses and orchestrate  them so yeah you can do that  there's direct callouts to do that if  you want to do it synchronously if you  want to do it asynchronously  you can do it with some of the built-in  programming languages for the custom  policies  the question here is a response to the  other one thank you for clarifying on  experience apis  how does it work with api facades do  they complement or supplement each other  so an experience api is really something  that's custom built to  support a particular application a  facade i would describe  as what you're doing is you're hiding  away the complexities of the enterprise  so you're separating the external  interface from the internal  implementation of that api  so think about how would you ideally  like to have this api look to the  outside world  and build an interface that looks like  that and then map that to the internal  services  and that can be done in a number of  different ways but really the difference  between these two  is the intention are we building this  for a specific  use or is this just a general purpose  way to improve  and simplify how our api is to call how  do you demonstrate the value of api  driven architecture within an enterprise  if they're happy with an esb  led architecture yeah that's a tough one  so  i would say once they get to see  something like this uh they would  absolutely love it  uh you know if i'm thinking old school  esp style architecture  uh where this would be better is think  about um  how long does it take you to develop an  application so if you wanted to write a  new  single page web application or a new  mobile application  what's that process like and how long  does it take to do something like that  also where's 